-- Lesson 1: What is a Fraction?
'{"hook":"Four thousand years ago, Egyptian scribes needed to divide loaves of bread fairly among workers building the pyramids. They invented fractions — and changed mathematics forever.","inventor":{"name":"Ahmes","birth_year":-1680,"death_year":-1620,"nationality":"Egyptian","bio":"An Egyptian scribe who copied the Rhind Mathematical Papyrus around 1650 BCE. This document, the oldest known mathematical textbook, contains extensive work with fractions and is our best window into how ancient Egyptians did mathematics."},"year_invented":-1800,"etymology":{"word":"fraction","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"fractio","meaning":"a breaking"},{"root":"frangere","meaning":"to break"}]},"story_paragraphs":["In ancient Egypt, building pyramids required feeding thousands of workers. Overseers had to divide bread, beer, and grain equally — but what if 3 loaves needed to be shared among 5 workers? Whole numbers weren''t enough.","The Egyptians developed a system of unit fractions — fractions with 1 as the numerator (like 1/2, 1/3, 1/4). They wrote any fraction as a sum of unit fractions: instead of 2/5, they wrote 1/3 + 1/15. The Rhind Papyrus, copied by scribe Ahmes around 1650 BCE, contains tables converting fractions this way.","Other civilizations developed their own approaches. Babylonians used base-60 fractions (which is why we have 60 minutes in an hour). Indian mathematicians were the first to write fractions the way we do today — with a numerator over a denominator — around 600 CE.","Today fractions are everywhere: cooking recipes (3/4 cup), music (a quarter note), probability (1 in 6 chance), and engineering measurements. Understanding fractions is the gateway to all higher mathematics."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Brahmagupta","contribution":"Indian mathematician (598-668 CE) who formalized rules for fraction arithmetic"},{"name":"al-Uqlidisi","contribution":"Arab mathematician who developed decimal fractions around 952 CE"},{"name":"Simon Stevin","contribution":"Flemish mathematician who popularized decimal notation in Europe in 1585"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Cooking","icon":"🍳","description":"Recipes use fractions constantly — 1/2 cup, 3/4 teaspoon, double or halve a recipe"},{"name":"Music","icon":"🎵","description":"Musical notes are fractions of a whole note — half notes, quarter notes, eighth notes"},{"name":"Construction","icon":"🔨","description":"Builders measure in fractions of inches — 3/8 inch, 5/16 inch"},{"name":"Probability","icon":"🎲","description":"The chance of rolling a 6 on a die is 1/6 — a fraction"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"The Rhind Mathematical Papyrus","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rhind_Mathematical_Papyrus.jpg"}}'::jsonb)
