-- Lesson 4: Introduction to Decimals
'{"hook":"Before 1585, there was no decimal point. Calculating 3.14 × 2.5 would have taken a page of fraction work. One Flemish engineer changed that with a 36-page pamphlet.","inventor":{"name":"Simon Stevin","birth_year":1548,"death_year":1620,"nationality":"Flemish (Belgian)","bio":"A Flemish mathematician and engineer who published De Thiende (The Tenth) in 1585, introducing decimal fractions to Europe. He argued that decimals were simpler than common fractions for trade, engineering, and science."},"year_invented":1585,"etymology":{"word":"decimal","from":"Latin","parts":[{"root":"decimus","meaning":"tenth"},{"root":"decem","meaning":"ten"}]},"story_paragraphs":["For thousands of years, merchants and scientists struggled with fractions. Multiplying 3/8 by 7/16 required finding common denominators and multiple steps. Trade calculations were slow and error-prone.","In 1585, Simon Stevin, a Flemish engineer and bookkeeper, published a tiny pamphlet called De Thiende (The Tenth). In just 36 pages, he showed that any fraction could be written using powers of ten — what we now call decimal notation.","Stevin''s idea was simple but revolutionary: extend the place-value system (ones, tens, hundreds) to the RIGHT of a marker. So 3 and 1/4 becomes 3.25 — three ones, two tenths, five hundredths. Multiplication and division became as easy as with whole numbers.","The decimal point spread across Europe within a century. Today every calculator, cash register, and computer uses decimals. Stevin''s 36 pages may be the highest impact-per-page ratio in the history of mathematics."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"al-Uqlidisi","contribution":"Arab mathematician who described decimal fractions around 952 CE, centuries before Stevin"},{"name":"John Napier","contribution":"Scottish mathematician who popularized the decimal point notation in 1614 alongside his logarithm tables"},{"name":"François Viète","contribution":"French mathematician who used decimals in trigonometric tables"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Money","icon":"💵","description":"Every currency uses decimals — $3.99, €12.50, £7.25"},{"name":"Measurement","icon":"📏","description":"Metric measurements are decimal — 1.5 meters, 0.75 kilograms"},{"name":"Science","icon":"🔬","description":"Scientific measurements use decimals — body temperature is 36.6°C"},{"name":"Computing","icon":"💻","description":"Computers process decimal numbers billions of times per second"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Title page of De Thiende by Simon Stevin, 1585","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Thiende_Simon_Stevin.jpg"}}'::jsonb)
